SPECS={
'00207':dict(title='Weekly cetuximab',context='Selected metastatic colorectal cancer; or locally advanced head/neck cancer with radiotherapy when platinum is unsuitable',setting='Advanced colorectal disease or curative head/neck radiotherapy',line='CRC monotherapy after the specified previous treatments; separate locally advanced head/neck indication',purpose='For selected bowel cancer that has spread, cetuximab aims to control the cancer after previous treatments. For selected locally advanced head-and-neck cancer when platinum chemotherapy is unsuitable, it is combined with radiotherapy. Your team confirms which pathway applies.',schedule='One larger first infusion is followed by weekly infusions. Bowel-cancer treatment continues while helping and tolerated. For head-and-neck treatment, cetuximab starts one week before radiotherapy and continues until radiation treatment finishes.',admin='The first infusion usually takes two hours and later infusions one hour. Extra observation is needed after the first two infusions. There is no home chemotherapy pump.',rationale='Cetuximab blocks EGFR signalling. RAS status selects metastatic colorectal treatment; it does not select the separate radiotherapy indication in squamous head/neck cancer.',baseline=['FBC, renal and liver profile; adequate marrow and organ function; ECOG 0–3.','Confirm indication: EGFR-expressing RAS wild-type mCRC with the exact prior-treatment conditions; or locally advanced squamous head/neck cancer with RT and platinum ineligibility.','Ask about previous antibody infusion reactions, red-meat allergy, tick bites and anti-cetuximab IgE results.'],cycle=['Regular FBC, renal and liver profile; review skin, nails, eyes, respiratory symptoms, hydration and electrolyte abnormalities.','Vital signs before, at one hour and after infusion; one-hour observation after doses 1 and 2. After two tolerated doses the source permits stopping that observation/vital-sign schedule.'],ongoing=['Monthly electrolytes, magnesium and calcium for two months after the last cetuximab dose; assign responsibility before treatment ends.','Response assessment follows the consultant plan; head/neck radiation follow-up is coordinated separately.'],cautions=['NCCP v6 uses conjunctive colorectal prior-treatment wording; uncertain histories require consultant confirmation.','Mutant or unknown RAS excludes the colorectal indication. Severe infusion reactions and confirmed ILD require discontinuation.','Bonner compared with radiotherapy alone, not cisplatin chemoradiation, and did not specifically enrol only platinum-ineligible patients.'],access=['Day-unit IV access; no home fluorouracil pump.','Head/neck pathway requires a separate radiation appointment and nutrition/swallowing support plan.']),
'00447':dict(title='Panitumumab + modified FOLFOX-6',context='First-line RAS wild-type metastatic colorectal cancer',setting='Advanced / metastatic colorectal cancer',line='First-line',purpose='This combination aims to control bowel cancer that has spread. A confirmed RAS wild-type result and your overall health help determine whether panitumumab is suitable.',schedule='A 14-day cycle: panitumumab, oxaliplatin, folinic acid and fluorouracil start on Day 1, followed by a fluorouracil pump lasting about 46 hours. Arrange disconnection around Day 3. Continue according to benefit and tolerance.',admin='Panitumumab is given before chemotherapy. Oxaliplatin and folinic acid are infused, then fluorouracil is given as a short dose and a separate 46-hour infusion. The team arranges central access, pump teaching and disconnection.',rationale='Panitumumab inhibits EGFR signalling in RAS wild-type cancer; oxaliplatin damages DNA and fluorouracil inhibits nucleotide synthesis. Folinic acid enhances fluorouracil activity and toxicity.',baseline=['Validated extended RAS wild-type result; confirm first-line metastatic indication, ECOG and organ function.','FBC, renal and liver profile; DPD test before fluorouracil. Interpret uracil cautiously in renal impairment and consider genotype confirmation.','Baseline electrolytes, magnesium and calcium; neuropathy, hearing/vision, skin and respiratory symptoms; access and pump assessment.'],cycle=['FBC, renal and liver profile before each cycle. Review diarrhoea, mucositis, neuropathy, infection, skin and infusion effects.','Check magnesium/calcium/potassium and correct abnormalities according to the local EGFR monitoring plan.'],ongoing=['Monthly electrolytes, magnesium and calcium for two months after the last panitumumab dose.','Assess cumulative oxaliplatin neuropathy and cold sensitivity throughout treatment and after stopping; disease scans follow the consultant plan.'],cautions=['Complete DPD deficiency excludes fluorouracil; partial deficiency requires individual starting-dose advice.','PRIME used FOLFOX4, not this exact modified FOLFOX-6 schedule; do not present its effect as schedule-specific.'],access=['Central venous access and a take-home fluorouracil pump with disconnect and emergency contacts.','Cold precautions and persistent neuropathy review are specific to the oxaliplatin component.']),
'00448':dict(title='Panitumumab + FOLFIRI',context='RAS wild-type metastatic colorectal cancer; first-line or selected second-line',setting='Advanced / metastatic colorectal cancer',line='First-line, or second-line after fluoropyrimidine-based therapy that excluded irinotecan',purpose='This combination aims to control bowel cancer that has spread and has a confirmed RAS wild-type result. The choice depends on previous treatment and your health.',schedule='A 14-day cycle: panitumumab, irinotecan, folinic acid and fluorouracil start on Day 1, followed by a fluorouracil pump for about 46 hours. Arrange disconnection around Day 3. Continue according to benefit and tolerance.',admin='Panitumumab is given before chemotherapy. Fluorouracil has a short dose and a separate 46-hour infusion. Irinotecan can cause an early cholinergic reaction and a different delayed-diarrhoea problem; both require an agreed plan.',rationale='Panitumumab inhibits EGFR signalling in RAS wild-type disease. Irinotecan inhibits topoisomerase I; fluorouracil affects nucleotide synthesis and folinic acid enhances its action.',baseline=['Validated extended RAS wild-type result and exact first-/second-line indication; review prior irinotecan exposure.','FBC, renal and liver profile; DPD test before fluorouracil. Renal impairment can confound uracil-based testing.','Baseline electrolytes, magnesium and calcium; bowel function, obstruction risk, skin, lung symptoms, medication interactions and pump readiness. ECG if cardiac function is compromised.'],cycle=['FBC, renal and liver profile before each cycle. Review early and delayed diarrhoea separately, hydration, fever, mouth soreness and skin/eye/respiratory symptoms.','Check magnesium/calcium/potassium and correct abnormalities according to the local EGFR monitoring plan.'],ongoing=['Monthly electrolytes, magnesium and calcium for two months after the last panitumumab dose.','Disease assessment follows the consultant plan. Escalate persistent diarrhoea, fever or dehydration between visits. Check INR if taking warfarin, as clinically indicated.'],cautions=['Complete DPD deficiency excludes fluorouracil; partial deficiency requires individual starting-dose advice.','UGT1A1 risk, liver dysfunction and interacting drugs require irinotecan-specific assessment; do not borrow oxaliplatin rules.','The numerical evidence presented is second-line; it does not quantify first-line benefit.'],access=['Central venous access, a 46-hour fluorouracil pump, planned disconnect and emergency contacts.','Provide written delayed-diarrhoea instructions separately from atropine for acute cholinergic symptoms.']),
'00568':dict(title='Pembrolizumab + pemetrexed + carboplatin',context='First-line metastatic non-squamous NSCLC without EGFR or ALK alterations',setting='Stage IV non-squamous NSCLC',line='First-line',purpose='The combination aims to control metastatic non-squamous lung cancer. Your team checks cancer markers, your health and your preferences. Chemotherapy and immunotherapy work in different ways.',schedule='Induction: all three medicines on Day 1 every 21 days for up to four cycles. Maintenance: carboplatin stops; pembrolizumab and pemetrexed may continue every 21 days while appropriate.',admin='Pembrolizumab is infused first over 30 minutes, pemetrexed over 10 minutes, and carboplatin over 30 minutes during induction only. Clinic time includes checks, preparation and support medicines.',rationale='Pembrolizumab blocks PD-1 immune signalling; pemetrexed inhibits folate-dependent nucleotide synthesis and carboplatin damages DNA. The planned maintenance phase removes carboplatin while retaining pembrolizumab and pemetrexed.',baseline=['FBC, renal/liver profile, glucose and thyroid function; measured isotope GFR preferred or validated GFR/CrCl estimate.','HBsAg, hepatitis B core antibody and hepatitis C screening. Confirm metastatic non-squamous histology, no EGFR/ALK alteration and ECOG 0–1.','Review autoimmune disease, pneumonitis, infection, immunosuppression, CNS disease, medicines and adequacy of folate/B12 supplementation.'],cycle=['FBC, renal/liver profile and glucose before every cycle in induction and maintenance. Pemetrexed requires CrCl at least 45 mL/min.','Thyroid function every 3–6 weeks; screen for immune toxicity every visit.','Assess ototoxicity and sensory neural damage before each platinum-containing cycle. Verify phase and stop carboplatin after induction.'],ongoing=['Folic acid continues throughout pemetrexed and for 21 days after its last dose. Vitamin B12 remains due every three cycles while pemetrexed continues.','Immune-related symptoms can arise during or after treatment; maintain alert-card and urgent-contact education. Response imaging follows the consultant plan.'],cautions=['Checkpoint treatment is held/discontinued by toxicity rules, not dose-reduced. Pemetrexed and carboplatin have separate modification rules.','KEYNOTE-189 allowed carboplatin or cisplatin and pembrolizumab up to 35 cycles; do not impose that trial cap as the universal NCCP duration.','Reconcile dexamethasone for pemetrexed rash prophylaxis with the induction antiemetic schedule; do not stack prescriptions.'],access=['IV treatment; no 46-hour home fluorouracil pump. Venous access choice is individual.','Check B12 appointments, daily folate supply, steroid directions and the immunotherapy alert card across both phases.']),
'00586':dict(title='Capecitabine + rectal radiotherapy',context='Locally advanced rectal cancer with concurrent radiotherapy',setting='Locally advanced rectal cancer',line='Concurrent chemoradiation within the agreed multimodality plan',purpose='Capecitabine tablets help pelvic radiotherapy work against locally advanced rectal cancer. Your team explains how this course fits with surgery and any other chemotherapy.',schedule='Take capecitabine twice daily on Days 1–5 of each 7-day cycle with radiotherapy, for a maximum of 5–6 cycles. Days 6–7 are not routine tablet days. Follow the team’s written instructions if radiotherapy or tablets are interrupted.',admin='Swallow tablets whole with water, with or within 30 minutes of food. Do not crush or cut them. The pharmacy supplies the prescribed tablet combination; do not calculate tablets from the mg/m² reference dose.',rationale='Capecitabine is converted to fluorouracil and sensitises tumour cells to radiation. Its dose and five-day weekly schedule differ from capecitabine systemic-therapy courses.',baseline=['FBC, renal and liver profile; DPD test before capecitabine. Complete DPD deficiency excludes treatment; partial deficiency needs specialist dosing.','Confirm locally advanced rectal indication, ECOG 0–1, pregnancy/lactation exclusion, oral adherence, interacting medicines and radiation plan.','Renal impairment can make uracil results difficult to interpret; consider genotype confirmation.'],cycle=['Weekly/pre-cycle FBC, renal and liver profile; assess diarrhoea, intake, hydration, hand-foot skin effects, mouth soreness and pelvic radiation toxicity.','Check actual tablet days against radiation appointments; record any hold/restart advice from the treating teams.'],ongoing=['Keep reviewing bowel, bladder, skin, fatigue and nutrition effects during and after the course. Late effects and surgical assessment follow the radiation/colorectal team plan.','Do not automatically extend tablets to make up missed radiation days or continue beyond the planned course.'],cautions=['The oral dose is twice daily on five days each week, not seven continuous treatment days.','Trials reflect complete rectal-cancer programmes including radiation and surgery; they do not isolate a survival gain caused by this tablet course.','Severe early toxicity, chest pain, dehydration or diarrhoea requires prompt assessment and the prescribed stop-and-call instructions.'],access=['No IV port or infusion pump is needed for capecitabine itself; separate treatments may differ.','Radiation appointments, tablet supply, meals and transport should be planned together.'])}

TRIALS={
'00207':('CO.17 KRAS subgroup; Bonner radiotherapy trial','https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18946061/','Previously treated KRAS wild-type metastatic CRC; separate locally advanced squamous head/neck population.','Cetuximab plus supportive care versus supportive care; separate cetuximab plus RT versus RT.','CO.17 used older KRAS testing. Head/neck evidence is separate and does not establish equivalence to platinum chemoradiation.',[('OS','CO.17 KRAS wild-type median OS','9.5 versus 4.8 months; HR 0.55 (95% CI 0.41–0.74).','Retrospective biomarker subgroup; group medians do not predict individual survival.')],'In selected previously treated bowel cancer, cetuximab improved disease control and survival compared with supportive care. Separate head-and-neck evidence supports adding it to radiotherapy in the appropriate setting.','Rash, infusion reactions and electrolyte loss matter; radiation adds its own local adverse effects.'),
'00447':('PRIME extended-RAS analysis','https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24024839/','512 patients with RAS wild-type metastatic colorectal cancer in a prospective-retrospective analysis.','Panitumumab + FOLFOX4 versus FOLFOX4, first-line.','FOLFOX4 differs from the NCCP modified FOLFOX-6 backbone; schedule-specific extrapolation is required.',[('PFS','Median progression-free survival','10.1 versus 7.9 months; HR 0.72 (95% CI 0.58–0.90), P=.004.','Progression or death; not an overall-survival measure.'),('OS','Median overall survival','26.0 versus 20.2 months; HR 0.78 (95% CI 0.62–0.99), P=.04.','Biomarker subgroup from the published 2013 analysis.')],'Adding panitumumab helped selected people with RAS wild-type bowel cancer control the cancer for longer. The chemotherapy schedule in the study differed from this exact regimen.','Panitumumab adds skin and electrolyte toxicity; chemotherapy adds neuropathy, infection and gastrointestinal risks.'),
'00448':('20050181 extended-RAS analysis','https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26341920/','Prospective-retrospective RAS analysis of second-line metastatic colorectal cancer.','Panitumumab + FOLFIRI versus FOLFIRI after one previous regimen.','The NCCP also includes first-line use, but these figures apply to the second-line analysis.',[('PFS','Progression-free survival hazard ratio','HR 0.70 (95% CI 0.54–0.91), P=.007.','Lower progression/death hazard in the RAS wild-type subgroup.'),('OS','Overall survival hazard ratio','HR 0.81 (95% CI 0.63–1.03), P=.08.','Not statistically significant; do not claim a proven OS improvement.'),('ORR','Objective response','41% versus 10%.','Tumour response is not cure or a survival rate.')],'For selected people whose bowel cancer had already received treatment, adding panitumumab improved tumour shrinkage and delayed progression. A longer overall survival was not established in this analysis.','Skin and electrolyte effects add to irinotecan diarrhoea, myelosuppression and fluorouracil toxicity.'),
'00568':('KEYNOTE-189 five-year update','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10082311/','616 untreated metastatic non-squamous NSCLC patients without EGFR/ALK alterations.','Pembrolizumab versus placebo, each with pemetrexed and four cycles of cisplatin or carboplatin, then pemetrexed maintenance.','Platinum choice was not carboplatin-only; the trial capped pembrolizumab at 35 cycles.',[('OS','Five-year overall survival','19.4% versus 11.3%; OS HR 0.60 (95% CI 0.50–0.72).','Five-year analysis; population estimates, not a personal forecast.'),('PFS','Progression-free survival hazard ratio','HR 0.50 (95% CI 0.42–0.60).','Progression or death; not identical to overall survival.')],'Adding pembrolizumab to chemotherapy helped people in the study live longer and delayed cancer progression. Your benefit depends on your cancer and health.','Immune-mediated toxicities can be serious and may persist or start after treatment; chemotherapy adds marrow, renal and gastrointestinal risks.'),
'00586':('NSABP R-04','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4849360/','1,608 stage II/III rectal cancer patients receiving preoperative chemoradiation, then surgery.','Capecitabine versus continuous-infusion fluorouracil in a factorial trial with or without oxaliplatin.','Results concern the full multimodality programme; trial schedule changed during recruitment. No isolated tablet-course survival benefit can be inferred.',[('Locoregional control','Three-year locoregional tumour events','Capecitabine 11.8% versus fluorouracil 11.2%; no significant difference.','Events are not synonymous with recurrence after complete resection.'),('OS','Five-year overall survival','Capecitabine 80.8% versus fluorouracil 79.9%; no significant difference.','Supports comparable outcomes, not capecitabine superiority.')],'Capecitabine was an effective oral alternative to infused fluorouracil when combined with radiotherapy in the study. Outcomes included the whole treatment programme, including surgery.','Bowel, skin and marrow toxicity require monitoring; adding oxaliplatin in this trial increased diarrhoea without improving the studied outcomes.')}

